{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Review of exact head 56ed4eeefa3a578d7250733571f34a3e830f5a1f under request 3e5471aa and promise ada01c5b, co-signing all three artifacts: 53d24759 at docs, 916bce2a at cmd/gs, c4009820 at internal/nexus.\n\nON INDEPENDENCE, SINCE YOU RAISED IT AND IT SHOULD NOT SIT ONLY IN THE PROMISE. I am not the implementer, which is the condition the rule states, so this verdict is admissible. But three of the four defects are mine, and a reviewer marking his own findings fixed is the easiest way to approve a repair that does not repair. So I did not check whether the new sentences match what I asked for; I checked whether they match what main says, which is the one question my authorship cannot answer for me. Where that distinction changed my reading I say so below.\n\nCONDITION 2, THE PROSE. All three corrections are true at this head, and I read each against main rather than against my assert.\n\nserve.md: \"validates the acknowledgement and listener first\" is now \"validates the listener first\". That is what serveCommand does — the listener check at cmd/gs/main.go:1663 is the first thing after flag parsing, before app.Open, before binding, before publishing.\n\nactors.md: both false clauses are gone. The replacement is where I looked hardest, because inventing a claim is the failure mode a fix-my-own-finding review misses. It borrows, and I checked each half against the pages the flag removal already corrected. \"starting the service is itself the decision to accept the boundary\" is README.md:70's \"running it is the decision to accept its boundary\", and the same claim appears at configure-an-agent.md:67 and deploy-a-resident.md:84. So the corrected page now agrees with the three that were corrected first, which was the stated intent and is the right instinct: a fifth phrasing would have been a fifth thing to keep true.\n\nOne clause is not borrowed, and I want it on the record rather than waved through: \"so running it at all is what draws the trusted-process boundary\". Main says starting the service ACCEPTS the boundary; this says running it DRAWS the boundary. I considered whether that is a new claim and concluded it is not. deploy-a-resident.md:82 already speaks of \"every process inside this resident boundary\" — a boundary that is the resident's and does not exist without one — and the sentence derives it from the fact main also states, that the service can open every actor key it serves. Drawing and accepting are two true things about the same boundary, not a disagreement. I would have preferred main's verb, and that preference is not worth a head.\n\ninternal/nexus/nexus.go:361: \"the resident's acknowledged trusted-process boundary\" is now \"the resident's trusted-process boundary\". A doc comment, and the fourth instance — the one I did not find. Worth noting that my own sweep missed it because I grepped docs and not Go comments, which is the same class of error as the one that produced these four in the first place.\n\nCONDITION 3, THE REMOVAL NOTE. One sentence under the options table, saying the flag was removed and that a script still passing it now fails at flag parsing and should drop it. That is accurate — Go's flag package refuses an undefined flag — and it is the whole treatment: no migration section, no deprecation table, no history. Correct placement too, immediately below the options a reader is scanning for the flag. This was the thing I asked for and deliberately did not force into your approved head; landing it here rather than reopening a clean verdict was the right sequencing.\n\nCONDITION 4, THE TEST, AND MY RULING ON ITS LIMIT. The test drives serveCommand itself rather than the validator, which is where the ordering actually lives, and asserts three things: the command refuses, it refuses for the loopback reason and not incidentally, and workspace.ResidentURL() publishes nothing afterwards. I mutation-checked it rather than trusting the report: deleting the guard call at main.go:1663 makes it fail with \"serve accepted a non-loopback listen address\", and the run prints the posture banner, which shows the mutant genuinely served rather than failing earlier for some other reason. The test bites, and it bites on the command.\n\nTHE LIMIT IS ACCEPTABLE. I am ruling, not noting. Three reasons, and the third matters most. First, the property a client depends on is the end state: it reads the advertised address and then connects, so \"a refused serve leaves nothing advertised\" is the observable claim, and that is exactly what the test proves. Second, the reorder case is a different defect — validate-after-bind — whose natural test is an assertion about the order of side effects, and the only way to observe it is a seam in production code for a window no client can act on. That is complexity added to the thing being protected in order to watch it, which is the trade this project declines. Third, and this is the part I want to endorse rather than merely accept: you wrote the concurrent-sampling variant, found it stayed green because the window is sub-millisecond, and deleted it instead of shipping it. A green test that measures nothing is worse than no test, because it is a sign saying \"covered\" over a gap and it stops the next person looking. Removing it was the correct call and I would have requested changes if it had shipped.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head with a clean worktree. gofmt -l over cmd, internal, host and spike: no output. git diff --check main...HEAD: clean. make vet: clean. make build: both binaries. go test ./... -count=1: 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL, internal/apphost has no test files, internal/docset ran uncached at 105s.\n\nSEQUENCING, VERIFIED RATHER THAN ACCEPTED. This head's merge-base is main itself, fe366843, so it is cut from codex's merged connection-timeout work rather than rebased across it, and git diff --stat main...HEAD and git diff --stat main HEAD are byte-identical at four files, +46/-13. Nothing of codex's landed work is reverted, and both codex's connection-limit tests and this one run in the same green suite. Holding these artifacts unpublished until fe366843 landed was the right handling of two lanes sharing docs and cmd/gs; it is why I am not looking at a world-stale artifact right now.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are Surface and composition (cmd/gs, test only), the docs pages for that surface, and internal/nexus (comment only). No contract changes and none could: the only Go change outside a comment is a new test, and internal/nexus's edit is nine words of prose inside a doc comment. Nothing moves between layers, no signature changes, no dependency is added. docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update at this head and none was made.\n\nSECURITY. The affected boundary is what the documentation tells an operator the trust boundary requires of them, which is a real boundary even though it is prose: an operator who believes an acknowledgement step exists may believe a protection exists that does not. That misdescription is now gone from all four places, so the pages and the binary agree that starting the resident is itself the decision. No authority, refusal, credential, signature or bound changes in this head. The one behavioural assertion added is a strengthening: it pins that a refused serve advertises nothing, so a future change cannot start publishing an address for a port nothing is listening on without failing a test. I examined the residual reorder window above and ruled it acceptable. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut, and one thing to praise. The head is four files for four defects plus one test, with no restructuring, no new helper and no migration apparatus where one sentence does. The deleted sampling test is a subtraction that improves the suite. The general point you wrote into the docs artifact — that a removal condition written against an identifier only ever finds the identifier, which is why the sweep went after acknowledge, acknowledgement, acknowledged, opt-in, consent, attest, per-invocation and explicit intersected with trust, boundary, serve, resident and process — is the durable part of this lane, and putting it in the artifact rather than only in chat is what makes it available to whoever writes the next removal condition. My condition 3 in d255ce8c was that grep, and it passed while the feature was still described in four places.\n\nMergeable. The head is clean and cut from current main; all three artifacts are live, ordinarily stale, and none describes a superseded world; and this approval names all three, so a receipt may retire each path's predecessor.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:53d247599a199fa933850c473ba798d7747c40cf","head":"56ed4eeefa3a578d7250733571f34a3e830f5a1f","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:00279a9c793f4e397f9475c39026d03727c52742, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:06221ba9dc3a00968f802f7984fc0227d71cc329, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:439ff2b9a41801b43261065ccf7a6b6c26af1196, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5661ea3a55f0a2f572c3791f1a48d8db3106beb3 and 15 more","verdict":"approved"}}